		<meta property="schema:genre">Fiction</meta>
		<dc:description id="description">In the post-Reconstruction era, a young Black man and woman from the deep South struggle to overcome the economic and political fleecing of their community.</dc:description>
		<meta id="long-description" property="se:long-description" refines="#description">
			&lt;p&gt;In post-Reconstruction Alabama, amidst the “silver fleece” of the cotton fields, a school for Black children attracts pupils eager to learn (but often unable to pay tuition)—and the hostility of plantation owners intent on maintaining their status and power. Among the pupils are Bles Alwyn, an ambitious and intelligent boy from Georgia, and Zora, an independent and mysterious Black girl from the nearby swamp. The story follows the efforts of Bless and Zora to help others in the working class by providing educational and economic opportunities that trap them in poverty: Bles by working within the political system, and Zora by building a collective that attempts to bypass the system.&lt;/p&gt;
			&lt;p&gt;&lt;i&gt;The Quest of the Silver Fleece&lt;/i&gt;, &lt;a href="https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/w-e-b-du-bois"&gt;W. E. B. Du Bois’s&lt;/a&gt; first novel, depicts in literary form many of the themes and insights from his earlier sociological masterpiece, &lt;a href="https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/w-e-b-du-bois/the-souls-of-black-folk"&gt;&lt;i&gt;The Souls of Black Folk&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt; (1903). Strikingly, Du Bois there anticipates this book, saying, “certainly one might frame a pretty and not far-fetched analogy of witchery and dragons’ teeth, and blood and armed men, between the ancient and the modern quest of the Golden Fleece”—between (a) the seemingly endless series of tests the mythical hero, Jason, overcomes (only through magic and the intervention of the gods) in order to return with the fleece of the golden ram so as to achieve his aim of wealth and social status, and (b) the seemingly endless series of economic hurdles Black sharecroppers would have to overcome in order to use the “silver fleece” of cotton to achieve their own aims of economic security and social equality. &lt;i&gt;The Quest of the Silver Fleece&lt;/i&gt; thus vividly portrays in literary form the interlocking educational, socio-economic, and political barriers in the deep South during the post-Reconstruction era, as well as the “Veil” that separates the races, ultimately offering a compelling vision of the importance of community and education to overcoming the “Negro Problem.”&lt;/p&gt;
		</meta>
